{#
  GENERATED FILE — page_builder_app_v2/shell.html is build output, not a source.
  Edit nav.njk / footer.njk / base.njk / post_chrome.njk instead; every site
  build refreshes it.

  It exists solely so the page builder's PREVIEW (/__pb/preview), its
  "Export HTML…" copy and its editor show the chrome the published page will
  get. Export does NOT use it: the builder writes a body fragment to
  input_custom_post/ and Eleventy wraps it with post_body.njk + base.njk at
  build time.

  The body below mirrors post_body.njk — header, content, ending, in that
  order, from the same post_chrome.njk macros — so preview and published page
  match by construction. Reorder one, reorder the other. The builder's
  tests/site-build.test.tsx builds both from these templates and compares them.

  Tokens the builder fills in, in a single pass (export.ts, assembleDocument):
    {{TITLE}}      page title, also in <title> and the og/twitter tags
    {{DATE}}       the date as readableDate prints it
    {{DATE_ISO}}   the date as isoStamp prints it, for datetime=""
    {{CONTENT}}    the exported body fragment
  and one marked region:
    <!--pb:header--> … <!--/pb:header-->   the header. Kept for a page without
                   a hero, removed for one with (it exports `header: false`).
  The builder refuses a shell without that region: it means shell.html predates
  this template, and the fix is to run the Eleventy build.

  KNOWN, DELIBERATE PREVIEW DIFFERENCE: the shell is rendered without
  `navScroll`, so the preview always shows the nav bar. A page that sets
  `navScroll: true` gets the hide-then-reveal nav once published.
#}
{% from "post_chrome.njk" import postHeaderBlock, postEnding %}
<!--pb:header-->
{{ postHeaderBlock("{{TITLE}}", "{{DATE_ISO}}", "{{DATE}}") }}
<!--/pb:header-->

{% raw %}{{CONTENT}}{% endraw %}

